-- statement:
--   For a natural number ℓ and a topological group G, proEllKernel is the intersection of all open normal subgroups H for which G/H is an ℓ-group; it is normal. ProEll(ℓ,F) is the quotient of the absolute Galois group of a field F by this kernel. For a topological group G, closedGammaThree is the closure of the commutator subgroup [closure([G,G]),G]; it is normal and contained in closure([G,G]). The groups Πᵃ(ℓ,F) and Πᶜ(ℓ,F) are respectively the topological abelianization of ProEll(ℓ,F) and its quotient by closedGammaThree. The containment defines a projection Πᶜ→Πᵃ with kernel Δ. Choosing representatives of x,y∈Πᵃ in ProEll defines a Δ-valued bracket as the image of x̃⁻¹ỹ⁻¹x̃ỹ; the representatives project back to x and y. A topological group isomorphism φ:Πᵃ(ℓ,L)→Πᵃ(ℓ,K) is bracket-compatible if some topological group isomorphism ψ:Δ(ℓ,L)→Δ(ℓ,K) carries every bracket [σ,τ] to [φ(σ),φ(τ)]. For field extensions K/k and L/l, PerfectIsom consists of ring isomorphisms between the perfect closures of K and L inside their algebraic closures that carry the embedded copy of k onto that of l. IsomF identifies these isomorphisms under the equivalence relation generated by β(x)=α(x)ᵖ, where p is prime and L has characteristic p. IsomC consists of the bracket-compatible isomorphisms φ above. When ℓ is prime, IsomCUnits identifies elements φ and χ under the equivalence relation generated by the existence of an ℓ-adic unit u such that, for every x, the powers φ(x) raised to the natural-number approximations of u converge to χ(x). The canonical map from an absolute Galois group to Πᵃ is the composite quotient map. PullbackGraph(α,φ) means that α extends to a ring isomorphism β between the algebraic closures and that, for every absolute Galois automorphism σ of L, there exists an absolute Galois automorphism τ of K with β∘τ=σ∘β and φ sending the class of σ to the class of τ. Finally, PhiGraph relates classes in IsomF and IsomCUnits precisely when they have representatives satisfying PullbackGraph; this defines a relation without asserting its functionality or injectivity.
